        ],
        "abstract": "Since the pioneering work by Oncley (1) on protein solutions, there have been a great deal of investigations on the dielectric behavior of macromolecules such as globular proteins and DNA. These studies established the electric moment and hydration in relation to the size and shape of these macromolecules. In recent years, however, the emphasis has been shifted to synthetic polypeptides and polyelectrolytes from natural biopolymers. Studies on synthetic polymers have been intended to elucidate the mechanism of electric polarization and its relaxation behavior as well as the hydrodynamic properties of the molecule. These detailed investigations are possible only when their structural units are already known. The use of simple model compounds is preferable for a complete understanding of all possible mechanisms of the dielectric relaxations. Because of stringent requirements imposed by the nature of the intended research, the use of simple model substances has been of particular interest.",
